{{!-- The publication's mark, at whatever size the caller's bar is.

The design draws the wordmark as type — the publication name in the display face, which
means it takes its colour from the text token and flips with the Mode for free. A Ghost
publication may have uploaded a logo instead, and a theme that ignores it makes a control in
admin do nothing, so the image wins when there is one.

An image does not flip. Ghost has exactly one logo setting, so a light-ink mark on MIRROR's
dark surface would be a mark nobody can see. `dark_mode_logo` is the way out for a buyer who
has one; a buyer who does not gets the publication name set in the display face for the dark
Mode only, which is the same word in the same face as the artwork it stands in for.

Params: `wrapper` (classes for the link), `logoHeight` (cap-height utility for the artwork,
not a font size), `titleSize` (type size for the text mark). --}}
<a
	href="{{@site.url}}"
	class="inline-flex shrink-0 items-center no-underline {{wrapper}}">
	{{#if @site.logo}}
		<img
			src="{{img_url @site.logo size="s"}}"
			alt="{{@site.title}}"
			class="{{logoHeight}} block w-auto dark:hidden">
		{{!-- Neither dark branch is hidden from assistive tech: `dark:hidden` above and `hidden`
		here are display:none, so exactly one of the two is ever in the tree, and whichever one it
		is has to be what names this link. Marking them decorative left the link with no
		accessible name at all in the dark Mode. --}}
		{{#if @custom.dark_mode_logo}}
			<img
				src="{{img_url @custom.dark_mode_logo size="s"}}"
				alt="{{@site.title}}"
				class="{{logoHeight}} hidden w-auto dark:block">
		{{else}}
			<span
				class="font-display tracking-display text-text hidden font-medium dark:block {{titleSize}}">{{@site.title}}</span>
		{{/if}}
	{{else}}
		<span
			class="font-display tracking-display text-text font-medium {{titleSize}}">{{@site.title}}</span>
	{{/if}}
</a>
